+    A mount can also be monitored for usage dropping below an expected
+    floor, which is useful for catching a mount that silently failed to
+    mount (in which case what looks like the mount point is really just an
+    empty directory on the root filesystem). To do this, use a dict with
+    ``min`` and/or ``max`` keys instead of a plain percentage. ``max``
+    behaves exactly like the plain percentage form above; ``min`` triggers
+    the beacon when usage falls below the given percentage. Both can be set
+    for the same mount, or different mounts can use different alerts.
+
+    .. code-block:: yaml
+
+        beacons:
+          diskusage:
+            - /: 63%
+            - /mnt/nfs:
+                min: 20%
+                max: 50%
+
     """
     whitelist = []
     config = salt.utils.beacons.remove_hidden_options(config, whitelist)
